    "referenceAnswerEn": "Environmental: development must respect the carrying capacity of the natural environment. Landfill space is limited, and leachate and landfill gas affect nearby land and air quality, so reducing the volume sent to landfill must be considered.\nEconomic: measures must be feasible and cost-effective. Recycling requires collection, sorting and processing facilities and labour, and when the market price of recovered materials is low the chain cannot pay for itself, so the economic sustainability of recycling must be considered.\nSocial: measures must be acceptable and fair. A waste charge falls proportionally harder on lower-income households, and facility siting often draws local objection, so fairness in sharing costs and communication with communities must be considered.\nAll three must be weighed together: attending to the environment alone leaves measures unaffordable to sustain, while attending to economics alone leaves them blocked at the implementation stage.",
    "contentEn": "Explain the three dimensions of sustainable development, and for each give one corresponding consideration in relation to Hong Kong’s municipal solid waste problem.",
