    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Labour market: the workforce shrinks as more leave than enter, reducing labour supply. With demand unchanged, shortages push up wage costs, and labour-intensive sectors — care, catering, construction — are hit first, with posts left unfilled.\nPublic finances: spending and revenue move adversely at once. Demand for healthcare and elderly services rises with the older population as recurrent expenditure, while the share of the population of taxpaying age falls and the salaries tax base narrows. Rising spending on a narrowing base creates more pressure than either factor alone.\n(b) Raising the retirement age — advantage: it directly enlarges the workforce and defers retirement benefits, helping both the labour market and public finances. Limitation: not all work suits older employees, physically demanding sectors cannot simply extend, and promotion prospects for younger staff may slow.\nExpanding labour importation — advantage: it acts quickly, filling shortages in specific sectors without waiting for the local age structure to change. Limitation: local workers fear wage pressure and fewer openings, and imported labour brings housing and social service needs, making it a population policy rather than a purely labour one.\nPromoting automation — advantage: it raises output per worker so production need not depend on workforce growth, a fundamental long-run direction. Limitation: high initial investment that smaller firms may not afford, some tasks (particularly personal care) resist automation, and displaced workers need retraining during the transition.\n(c) Group one: middle-aged manual workers. Group two: clerical or professional employees.\nTake raising the retirement age. For the second group it means more years of income and a longer career, broadly positive. For the first, physical capacity declines with age, so a later retirement age can mean continuing to work in worsening physical condition, with no lighter post available in that trade.\nThe same measure differs across groups because the measure is uniform while the nature of the work, physical demands, bargaining power and available alternatives are not. Any population or labour policy must therefore be assessed by asking on whom it falls, not by aggregate figures alone.",
    "contentEn": "Hong Kong’s population continues to age, with the share aged 65 and over expected to rise substantially over the next twenty years.\n(a) Identify one challenge for the labour market and one for public finances, explaining the mechanism in each case.\n(b) Three measures are under consideration: raising the statutory retirement age, expanding labour importation, and promoting automation. Give one advantage and one limitation of each.\n(c) Identify two stakeholder groups affected to different degrees, and explain why one measure produces different effects across groups.",
    "explanationEn": "Part (a) asks for mechanisms, not observations. \"The workforce shrinks\" is the observation; \"more leave than enter, supply falls, shortages raise wage costs\" is the mechanism. For public finances the key is that spending and revenue move adversely at the same time — answering only one side omits half the problem.\nPart (c) separates candidates most and tests what marking in this subject values: one policy falling differently on different people. Define the two groups explicitly and as specifically as possible, analyse one measure across both, then draw the general point that the policy is uniform while circumstances are not. Saying vaguely that some gain and some lose, without naming who or why, does not score well.",
    "markingScheme": "（a）4 分 —— 勞動市場的挑戰連機制（2）；公共財政的挑戰連機制（2）。公共財政一項須同時提及開支上升與稅基收窄，只答一面給 1 分。\n（b）3 分 —— 三項措施各 1 分，每項須有優點及限制各一；只有其中一項者不給該分。\n（c）2 分 —— 明確界定兩個群體（1）；以同一項措施說明效果差異，並歸納出處境不同的原則（1）。泛泛而談不給分。\n本題所列分數為本平台練習用尺度，並非香港考試及評核局的分數分配。參考答案只列必達步驟與判準，刻意不提供可直接抄錄的完整答卷。交卷後請逐項對照自評，機器不會為本題評分。",
    "markingSchemeEn": "(a) 4 marks — the labour market challenge with its mechanism (2); the public finance challenge with its mechanism (2). The finance point must cover both rising spending and a narrowing tax base; one side alone earns 1.\n(b) 3 marks — one per measure, each needing both an advantage and a limitation; only one of the two earns nothing for that measure.\n(c) 2 marks — two groups defined explicitly (1); one measure analysed across both with the general point drawn (1). Generalities earn nothing.\nThe marks shown are a practice scale used on this platform, not an HKEAA mark allocation. The reference answer lists the required steps and criteria rather than a copyable model script. Compare your own work against it point by point; nothing here is machine-marked.",
    "suggestedMinutes": 16
